Scott asks that if \(\mathcal{D}_0\) and \(\mathcal{D}_1\) are neighbourhood systems over \(\Delta_0,\Delta_1\subseteq\Sigma^*\) with no neighbourhood empty, then \(\mathcal{D}_0+\mathcal{D}_1=\{\{\Lambda\}\cup 0\Delta_0\cup 1\Delta_1\}\cup\{0X\mid X\in\mathcal{D}_0\}\cup\{1Y\mid Y\in\mathcal{D}_1\}\) is a neighbourhood system over \(\{\Lambda\}\cup 0\Delta_0\cup 1\Delta_1\), and that there are maps \(in_i\) and \(out_i\) with \(out_i\circ in_i=I_{\mathcal{D}_i}\); he also asks why forbidding the empty neighbourhood is harmless, where that hypothesis is used, how to pass to an \(n\)-fold sum on the carriers \(1^i0\Delta_i\), and he records that the concrete prefixes make \(\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{B}+\mathcal{B}\) as an equality of sets, while the request to draw pictures is not a formal claim. The Lean carrier is `Option (α ⊕ β)`: `il` and `ir` send a token to `some (inl ·)` and `some (inr ·)`, `none` stands for \(\Lambda\), `inj₀` and `inj₁` are the images of those tags, and `sumMaster` is `insert none (inj₀ Δ₀ ∪ inj₁ Δ₁)`. Given `h₀` and `h₁`, that every neighbourhood is nonempty, `sum` takes as neighbourhoods exactly `sumMaster`, the left copies of neighbourhoods of the first system, and the right copies of neighbourhoods of the second. Containment in the master is `inj₀_subset_sumMaster` and `inj₁_subset_sumMaster`. The intersection proof in `sum` cases on the three forms: the master absorbs a copy by `sumMaster_inter_inj₀` and `sumMaster_inter_inj₁`; two left copies meet in `inj₀` of the intersection after `inj₀_inter` and `inj₀_subset_inj₀` push the witness down, a master witness being impossible by `none_mem_inj₀` and a right witness by `ir_mem_inj₀` together with nonemptiness of the right neighbourhood; two right copies are the symmetric argument with `inj₁_inter`, `none_mem_inj₁`, and `il_mem_inj₁`; a left copy meets a right copy in the empty set by `inj₀_inter_inj₁`, and `inj₀_nonempty` together with `inj₁_nonempty` makes every consistency witness nonempty, so that witness cannot lie in the empty intersection. Reading \(0\), \(1\), and \(\Lambda\) as `inl`, `inr`, and `none`, that is the displayed neighbourhood system. The maps are approximable relations: `inMap₀` and `inMap₁` hold when the tagged copy is contained in the sum neighbourhood, and `outMap₀` and `outMap₁` hold when `leftPart` or `rightPart` is contained in the output neighbourhood. `leftPart` is the preimage under `il`, enlarged by all of \(\Delta_0\) when the sum set meets a right tag or `none`; `leftPart_inj₀` recovers the underlying set on a left copy, and `leftPart_inj₁` together with `leftPart_sumMaster` send a nonempty right copy and the sum master to \(\Delta_0\), with the right-hand lemmas symmetric. `outMap₀_comp_inMap₀` is extensionality of approximable maps: a composite witness is a sum neighbourhood containing `inj₀ X` whose left content sits in \(Z\), so \(X\) sits in that left content and hence in \(Z\), while \(X\subseteq Z\) is witnessed by the neighbourhood `inj₀ X` itself through `leftPart_inj₀`; `outMap₁_comp_inMap₁` repeats the argument on the right, and `example_318` is the conjunction of the two identities. Emptiness is used to keep those witnesses nonempty and to make the opposite projection equal \(\Delta_i\) in `leftPart_inj₁` and `rightPart_inj₀`, which is the place the hypothesis enters the construction. **Theorem:** partial — `sum` is the displayed system and `example_318` is the two retract identities, while the claim that empty neighbourhoods may be deleted without changing the domain, the set equality \(\mathcal{B}=\mathcal{B}+\mathcal{B}\), and the \(n\)-ary sum are absent. **Proof:** partial — the intersection cases and the two retract calculations establish the system and the identities for the tagged carrier, and they leave the deletion argument, the concrete set equality, and the \(n\)-ary extension unproved.
